  "about": [
    "Timucua was spoken in northern Florida and southern Georgia, in the towns the Spanish called Timucua, Potano, Mocama (the coast), Acuera, Agua Salada and Agua Fresca, among others. Each town had its own variety.",
    "It is a language isolate: no relatives are known. Epidemics, wars and the Spanish departure from Florida in 1763 ended its last speakers, who left for Cuba.",
    "We know it from the books of the Franciscan Francisco Pareja, who lived among the Timucua at San Juan del Puerto: his 1614 grammar, catechisms and a bilingual confessional. It is one of the first languages of what is now the United States to be written down."
  ],
  "orthography": {
    "intro": "Pareja wrote Timucua with Spanish letters. Here we use a new Mati Mati spelling: k for /k/, q for /kʷ/, w and y for the glides, h for the sound Pareja wrote h, g, x or j, a macron for a long vowel (ō) and an acute for stress that doesn't fall where expected (pwenohekó).",
