    "notes": [
      "Every word ends in a vowel; Pareja found only one exception, unan. The only consonant clusters are nt and st.",
      "q and k change the meaning: ikino «go up», iqino «kill»; hikino «dream», hiqino «finish».",
      "Words of up to three vowels are stressed on the first one (téra «good»); longer ones never on the last. Stress is only written when it falls at the end: elakó «and the sun», pwenohekó «will he come?».",
      "When one word ends in a vowel and the next begins with one, one of them is lost: terati chi inela → teratichinela.",
      "The pronunciation is reconstructed from Pareja's spelling; nobody has spoken Timucua since the 18th century."
    ]
  },
